WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.863
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:08.607
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.907 --> 00:00:15.216
Unit twenty-four: Water Governance. Today you will describe disputes using complex noun phrases.

4
00:00:15.516 --> 00:00:18.204
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.574
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:24.592
Water governance.

7
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:30.381
Water governance means the rules and institutions that manage who uses water and how.

8
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:33.923
Water governance decides who gets water in a drought.

9
00:00:34.517 --> 00:00:37.517
Your turn. Say: Water governance.

10
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:41.751
Water governance.

11
00:00:42.517 --> 00:00:43.715
River basin.

12
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:49.317
River basin means the whole area drained by a river and its tributaries.

13
00:00:49.917 --> 00:00:52.392
The river basin spans six countries.

14
00:00:52.983 --> 00:00:56.042
Your turn. Say: River basin.

15
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.296
River basin.

16
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.190
Allocation.

17
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:07.191
Allocation means the distribution of a limited resource among users.

18
00:01:07.783 --> 00:01:10.294
The allocation of water favoured farmers.

19
00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:13.733
Your turn. Say: Allocation.

20
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:17.765
Allocation.

21
00:01:18.517 --> 00:01:19.773
Transboundary.

22
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:23.967
Transboundary means crossing the borders between countries.

23
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:27.512
Transboundary rivers require shared agreements.

24
00:01:28.117 --> 00:01:30.862
Your turn. Say: Transboundary.

25
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.073
Transboundary.

26
00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:36.504
Dam.

27
00:01:37.517 --> 00:01:39.969
Dam means a barrier built to hold back water.

28
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:43.883
The dam generates power but alters the river's flow.

29
00:01:44.483 --> 00:01:47.554
Your turn. Say: Dam.

30
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.297
Dam.

31
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.225
Cooperation.

32
00:01:54.217 --> 00:01:57.180
Cooperation means working together toward a shared aim.

33
00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:01.536
Cooperation between upstream and downstream states is essential.

34
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.116
Your turn. Say: Cooperation.

35
00:02:07.967 --> 00:02:09.119
Cooperation.

36
00:02:09.883 --> 00:02:10.942
Scarcity.

37
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.437
Scarcity means a shortage of something needed.

38
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.909
Seasonal scarcity strained the city's supply.

39
00:02:18.517 --> 00:02:21.157
Your turn. Say: Scarcity.

40
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.396
Scarcity.

41
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.360
Stakeholders.

42
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.743
Stakeholders means people or groups with an interest in a decision.

43
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.429
Stakeholders included farmers, fishers and cities.

44
00:02:36.017 --> 00:02:38.890
Your turn. Say: Stakeholders.

45
00:02:41.733 --> 00:02:42.862
Stakeholders.

46
00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:47.271
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:48.117 --> 00:02:53.056
Visal, why do countries along the Mekong quarrel over dams? Water is water.

48
00:02:54.150 --> 00:03:02.512
The allocation of water between neighbouring countries is contested, because upstream decisions shape downstream livelihoods.

49
00:03:03.617 --> 00:03:06.185
Isn't more power from dams good for everyone?

50
00:03:07.283 --> 00:03:15.297
The construction of upstream dams brings energy, but the reduction in seasonal flooding threatens fisheries and farmland downstream.

51
00:03:16.383 --> 00:03:18.035
How can this be resolved?

52
00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:27.584
Through the negotiation of binding agreements and the inclusion of all stakeholders. The establishment of shared monitoring would build trust.

53
00:03:28.733 --> 00:03:30.141
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.983 --> 00:03:33.900
Grammar focus: Complex noun phrases.

55
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.399
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.083 --> 00:03:39.801
The allocation of water between neighbouring countries is contested.

57
00:03:40.717 --> 00:03:44.167
The construction of upstream dams threatens downstream fisheries.

58
00:03:45.083 --> 00:03:48.128
The establishment of shared monitoring would build trust.

59
00:03:49.067 --> 00:04:00.438
Build dense noun phrases: the + nominalisation + of + noun (+ between / in + noun). The head noun (allocation) decides verb agreement: The allocation … is contested.

60
00:04:01.183 --> 00:04:05.519
Be careful! Don't say: The allocation water is contested.

61
00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:10.093
Say: The allocation of water is contested.

62
00:04:10.393 --> 00:04:12.160
allocation of + noun.

63
00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:15.793
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.240
Rivers Without Borders.

65
00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:24.103
Water governance concerns the rules by which societies share a resource that cannot be replaced.

66
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:31.639
The allocation of water between neighbouring countries is contested, particularly in river basins that cross several borders.

67
00:04:32.633 --> 00:04:40.542
The construction of upstream dams brings electricity and irrigation, but the reduction in seasonal flooding can threaten fisheries and farms downstream.

68
00:04:41.533 --> 00:04:49.001
Transboundary cooperation therefore depends on the exchange of data, the inclusion of all stakeholders and the creation of dispute mechanisms.

69
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.496
Where scarcity grows, the temptation to act unilaterally also grows.

70
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.915
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.911
The allocation of water between countries blank contested.

72
00:05:04.211 --> 00:05:07.283
A, is. B, are. C, being.

73
00:05:10.833 --> 00:05:12.450
The answer is A: is.

74
00:05:13.583 --> 00:05:17.057
The area drained by a river and its tributaries is a blank.

75
00:05:17.357 --> 00:05:21.603
A, river basin. B, dam. C, stakeholder.

76
00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:27.468
The answer is A: river basin.

77
00:05:28.617 --> 00:05:30.988
Crossing the borders between countries is blank.

78
00:05:31.288 --> 00:05:35.313
A, transboundary. B, scarce. C, local.

79
00:05:38.867 --> 00:05:41.470
The answer is A: transboundary.

80
00:05:42.617 --> 00:05:44.500
Which is a complex noun phrase?

81
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:50.462
A, Water is shared.. B, The construction of upstream dams. C, They build dams..

82
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.219
The answer is B: The construction of upstream dams.

83
00:05:58.383 --> 00:06:02.438
According to the reading, where scarcity grows, so does the temptation to…

84
00:06:02.738 --> 00:06:07.413
A, cooperate. B, act unilaterally. C, build schools.

85
00:06:10.967 --> 00:06:13.814
The answer is B: act unilaterally.

86
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.059
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.917 --> 00:06:21.903
Why is transboundary water cooperation difficult?

88
00:06:38.717 --> 00:06:40.240
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.540 --> 00:07:06.778
Cooperation is difficult because the interests of upstream and downstream countries differ. Upstream states want energy and irrigation, whereas downstream states depend on steady seasonal flows. Trust is limited when data are not shared, and national sovereignty makes enforcement hard. Binding agreements and shared monitoring are therefore essential.

90
00:07:07.078 --> 00:07:08.845
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:13.958
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and complex noun phrases.

92
00:07:14.258 --> 00:07:19.499
Today's value is peaceful cooperation. Share resources through patience and dialogue.

93
00:07:19.799 --> 00:07:22.765
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:23.065 --> 00:07:25.741
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

